        <figcaption id="optimization-caption">Acceleration is layered around an exact reference path rather than substituted for it.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="article-body">
        <p class="article-opening">
          “Make vector search faster” sounds like one task. In practice it is a
          stack of different problems: examine fewer vectors, make each comparison
          cheaper, keep data close to the CPU, avoid rebuilding unchanged work, and
          use parallel hardware where its overhead is justified.
        </p>

        <p>
          Each optimization also introduces a new way to be wrong. Approximate
          traversal can miss neighbors. Quantization changes scores. Parallel graph
          construction can become nondeterministic. A GPU cache can be stale while
          writes continue on the CPU. Even a faster query is a regression if it
          silently returns worse results.
        </p>

        <p>
          I designed FocalVector around that tension. Exact search remains the
          correctness oracle. Benchmarks measure recall against it, durable APIs
          rerank with authoritative full-precision vectors, and accelerated state
          remains rebuildable. That gives every optimization a reference answer and
          a clear failure boundary.
        </p>

        <aside class="article-callout">
          <span>The performance rule</span>
          <p>An optimization is only a win when its cost, recall, and failure behavior are all visible.</p>
        </aside>

        <h2 id="hnsw">First, avoid most comparisons.</h2>

        <p>
          Exact search scores every eligible vector. It is simple, deterministic,
          and indispensable for validation, but its work grows linearly with the
          collection. FocalVector’s primary approximate index is HNSW: a layered
          proximity graph that navigates toward promising regions instead of
          scanning the full corpus.
        </p>

        <p>
          There is no single “HNSW speed.” Graph density (<code>M</code>), build
          breadth (<code>ef_construction</code>), and query breadth
          (<code>ef_search</code>) spend memory and computation at different times.
          On a deterministic one-million-vector, 128-dimensional cosine corpus, a
          high-recall graph built with <code>M=32</code> and
          <code>ef_construction=400</code> reached 96.0% recall@10 at a mean 2.430 ms
          with <code>ef_search=384</code>. Raising query breadth to 768 reached 97.7%
          at 4.827 ms. The curve matters more than either endpoint.
        </p>

        <p>
          An important lesson from that benchmark was that query effort cannot
          recover information the graph never captured. A faster-to-build
          <code>M=16</code> graph reached only 85.7% recall even at
          <code>ef_search=1536</code>. Construction quality, resident memory, and
          query latency are one coupled decision.
        </p>

        <h2 id="quantization">Then, make graph traversal cheaper.</h2>

        <p>
          HNSW reduces the number of candidates, but graph traversal still performs
          many vector comparisons. Storing every traversal vector as
          <code>f32</code> costs four bytes per component and moves correspondingly
          more data through the cache hierarchy. FocalVector’s durable indexes use
          symmetric scalar-int8 quantization: one signed byte per component, plus a
          per-vector scale and cached squared norm.
        </p>

        <p>
          Quantization is used to navigate, not to define the final answer. A query
          traverses the graph with quantized cosine, dot-product, or Euclidean
          scores, oversamples four times the requested result count, then reranks
          those candidates against the authoritative full-precision vectors.
          Filtered exact search and the recent-write delta remain full precision as
          well. Returned durable-API scores therefore retain <code>f32</code>
          semantics even though the fast path used approximate arithmetic to find
          candidates.
        </p>
      </div>

