      <div class="article-body">
        <h2 id="simd">SIMD turns compact data into useful throughput.</h2>

        <p>
          The compact representation creates an opportunity for wider arithmetic.
          On x86-64, FocalVector runtime-detects AVX2 and processes 16 signed-byte
          component pairs at a time. It widens them to 16-bit lanes, multiplies and
          accumulates into 32-bit lanes, then finishes any tail with the portable
          implementation. The dispatch is guarded at runtime, so the persisted
          format is not tied to one CPU instruction set.
        </p>

        <p>
          Two details matter here. First, the accumulator path is bounded: vectors
          above the safe AVX2 length use the checked portable implementation.
          Second, tests compare the vectorized and portable results at boundary
          lengths and extreme values. SIMD is an implementation choice, not a new
          scoring definition.
        </p>

        <h2 id="locality">The quiet wins: locality and allocation control.</h2>

        <p>
          Arithmetic was only part of the cost. The original graph shape could also
          lose time chasing allocations. FocalVector now stores vector components in
          contiguous arenas. After construction, neighbor lists are flattened into
          compact ranges over one <code>u32</code> link array, and point IDs live in
          a single ordinal table instead of being duplicated in every graph node.
        </p>

        <p>
          Graph traversal uses generation-marked visitation arrays rather than
          allocating and hashing a fresh visited set for each search. Query
          workspaces come from a bounded, contention-safe pool and return after use.
          The same generation-marked workspaces are reused by construction workers.
          None of these changes is as dramatic as “GPU acceleration,” but together
          they reduce allocator traffic and give the CPU more predictable memory
          access.
        </p>

        <h2 id="parallel-build">Parallelize construction without randomizing it.</h2>

        <p>
          HNSW insertion is order-sensitive, which makes naïve parallel construction
          both difficult to reproduce and difficult to debug. FocalVector builds a
          4,096-point seed graph serially, then plans groups of 512 insertions in
          parallel against a stable graph snapshot. Plans are applied in input order.
        </p>

        <p>
          Rayon supplies the worker pool, but scheduling does not decide the graph.
          Stable input ordering, deterministic levels, stable score tie-breaking,
          and ordered plan application produce the same graph across worker counts.
          This preserves reproducible benchmarks and storage while using available
          cores for the expensive planning phase.
        </p>

        <h2 id="persistence">Stop copying and rebuilding unchanged data.</h2>

        <p>
          Query speed can be undermined by what happens between queries. FocalVector
          recovers immutable segments through read-only memory maps. Persisted int8
          graph codes and aligned full-precision vectors are scored directly from
          mapped files instead of being copied into second heap arenas. An owned
          public point is materialized lazily only when an ID is explicitly fetched;
          search and reranking stay zero-copy.
        </p>

        <p>
          Writes use another form of work avoidance. Recent mutations remain in a
          small exact-search delta and merge with results from immutable graphs.
          Routine flushes build a new segment only for changed live IDs rather than
          rebuilding the entire collection. In the repository benchmark, updating
          1,000 points in a 100k × 128 collection produced a 0.83 MB segment instead
          of 83.6 MB and reduced flush time from 8.73 seconds to 121.6 milliseconds.
          Search fans out across at most eight segments; the next flush compacts
          them into a new full graph.
        </p>

        <h2 id="filters">A filter should reduce work, not destroy recall.</h2>

        <p>
          Applying metadata filters after a small ANN result set is fast but wrong:
          it can return fewer than <code>k</code> results even when enough qualifying
          points exist. FocalVector builds equality and numeric-range indexes for
          immutable snapshots, estimates selectivity, and chooses between exact
          candidate scoring, filtered graph traversal, and broader HNSW search.
        </p>

        <p>
          For selective filters, an indexed exact scan can be cheaper and more
          reliable than graph traversal. For broad filters, HNSW remains useful as
          long as traversal can expand when the filtered result set is too small.
          The optimization is the plan choice—not a blanket assumption that ANN is
          always faster.
        </p>

        <h2 id="cuda">CUDA is an optional exact query engine.</h2>

        <p>
          FocalVector can also keep an immutable full-precision matrix on an NVIDIA
          GPU. For an unfiltered query, it uploads one vector and uses cuBLAS GEMV to
          compute dot products against the snapshot. Cosine, dot-product, and
          Euclidean scoring then follow the collection’s existing semantics.
        </p>

        <p>
          This path is intentionally optional at two levels. CUDA support is a Cargo
          feature with dynamically loaded CUDA 12 driver and cuBLAS libraries, so a
          normal build has no CUDA dependency. At runtime, CUDA can be off, preferred
          above a configurable collection-size threshold, or required. Preferred mode
          falls back to CPU/HNSW when initialization fails or the collection is too
          small to amortize device overhead.
        </p>

        <p>
          The device matrix is a rebuildable cache, never durable state. After a
          flush, FocalVector refreshes it from authoritative points. Mutations newer
          than the GPU snapshot remain on the exact CPU delta path; dirty IDs are
          excluded from device results and both candidate lists are merged. Filtered
          searches continue through metadata planning and the CPU engine.
        </p>

        <p>
          The current baseline still copies one score per snapshot vector back to
          the host for deterministic top-k selection and full-precision reranking.
          Device-side top-k reduction is the obvious next optimization for larger
          collections. Memory is another honest constraint: one million
          128-dimensional <code>f32</code> vectors occupy about 488 MiB of VRAM before
          scores and cuBLAS workspaces. CUDA is a query option to benchmark, not a
          badge that automatically makes every workload faster.
        </p>

        <blockquote>
          HNSW avoids work. Quantization and SIMD cheapen work. Memory mapping avoids
          duplicate work. CUDA moves exact work. The oracle decides whether any of it
          was correct.
        </blockquote>

        <h2 id="lesson">The optimization stack is the product.</h2>

        <p>
          FocalVector’s fastest path is not one algorithm. It is coordination among
          representations and execution engines: quantized graph traversal finds a
          candidate set, full-precision vectors settle the ranking, metadata indexes
          change the plan, a CPU delta preserves fresh writes, and an optional GPU
          snapshot accelerates the cases that fit it.
        </p>

        <p>
          That layering is what keeps the system understandable. Each fast path has
          a workload where it wins, a boundary where it stops, and an exact path to
          compare against. Performance engineering becomes a series of measurable,
          reversible decisions instead of one irreversible bet.
        </p>
      </div>

